WEBVTT

00:00:00.174 --> 00:00:01.784
What if India had two time zones?

00:00:02.039 --> 00:00:04.409
Right now, the whole
country runs on one clock.

00:00:04.935 --> 00:00:07.205
But India is nearly three
thousand kilometres wide,

00:00:07.615 --> 00:00:11.325
and the sun rises almost two hours
earlier in the northeast than in Gujarat.

00:00:11.590 --> 00:00:14.120
In Assam, summer mornings
are bright before five.

00:00:14.141 --> 00:00:15.951
Offices open hours after daylight.

00:00:16.051 --> 00:00:19.461
Tea gardens already run their
own "garden time", an hour ahead.

00:00:19.487 --> 00:00:21.517
Two zones could mean
more daylight at work,

00:00:21.687 --> 00:00:24.037
and some studies say
lower electricity use.

00:00:24.117 --> 00:00:25.527
Russia uses eleven zones.

00:00:25.787 --> 00:00:28.297
China, just as wide as India, uses one.

00:00:28.303 --> 00:00:29.213
But there'd be costs.

00:00:29.408 --> 00:00:32.058
Train timetables get tricky
at the border between zones.

00:00:32.339 --> 00:00:34.649
Flights, banks and exams
would need new rules.

00:00:34.774 --> 00:00:38.464
And on busy single-line tracks,
a confused clock can be dangerous.

00:00:38.664 --> 00:00:41.290
One clock keeps a billion
people on the same minute.

00:00:41.290 --> 00:00:43.080
So what if India had two time zones?

